# Design protection (highest priority rule)

The visual design, style and feel of this website are a core business asset. No task — performance, security, accessibility, refactoring, testing, dependency updates — may reduce its aesthetics or change how it looks or behaves for the user without my explicit approval.

## Never change without my explicit approval
- Colors, gradients, design tokens (src/styles/tokens.css, globals.css, galaxy.css)
- Fonts, font sizes, weights, line heights, letter spacing
- Spacing, layout, grid, alignment, component sizes, border radius, shadows
- Animations and transitions (motion, gsap): timing, easing, sequence, triggers
- The 3D scene (three.js / React Three Fiber): composition, materials, lighting, post-processing, camera, quality
- Hover, focus and scroll effects, micro-interactions
- Images, icons, illustrations and their quality or cropping
- Texts and copy in all locales
- RTL layout for Arabic

## Never do
- Remove, simplify, shorten or disable any animation or visual effect to gain performance.
- Lower image or 3D quality (resolution, antialiasing, post-processing) to gain performance.
- Replace custom UI components with generic or library components.
- Add a new UI, CSS or animation library.
- Update baseline screenshots to make a failing visual test pass.

## Allowed without approval (only if visually identical)
- Changing WHEN or HOW something loads (lazy loading, code splitting, caching, rendering strategy), as long as the final visual result is identical and there is no visible flash, jump or layout shift.

## Required process for any change that could affect visuals
1. Visual regression screenshots (Playwright toHaveScreenshot) for key pages in en and ar, desktop and mobile, must pass unchanged.
2. If a visual change seems necessary (for example an accessibility contrast fix, or a security header that blocks an effect), STOP. Show me before/after screenshots and 2-3 options, starting with the smallest visual change, and wait for my decision.
3. If a target (performance score, accessibility rule) cannot be met without a visual change, report the trade-off and the best result achievable without it. The design wins unless I decide otherwise.
